## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE STALLKEEPERS — the market's ambient conversation (the `ST` tree)
##
## Nine quips (`ST1`-`ST9`, `story.ni:1881-1935`) that every stallkeeper in
## Grubber's speaks. In the source they hang off `Rule for initiating
## conversation with a stallkeeper`, so the tree belongs to the KIND, not to a
## character — which is what makes it worth carrying whole (change document,
## "The stallkeeper tree"): nine quips of texture that apply everywhere the walk
## goes, at no per-character authoring cost.
##
## WHY THIS IS ITS OWN FILE. Chord binds a conversation to an entity —
## `define topics for <entity>` needs a person and takes exactly one owner
## (`packages/chord/src/analyzer.ts:1509, 1520`), and `define greetings` and
## `define exchange` are owner-bound the same way. A tree shared by ten
## characters therefore has no shared home: it is ten near-identical blocks
## around one set of shared phrases. The PROSE cost is still zero, which is what
## the change document promised — but the structural cost is real, and it is
## recorded as watch-list item W-8 rather than hidden here.
##
## WHAT THE REWRITE CHANGED, and what it did not:
##
##   * Gentry's menu becomes topics. `ST2`/`ST3`/`ST4` are menu choices in 2009;
##     here they are things Jack can ask about, because Chord is a parser game
##     and a menu is not the idiom. The words spoken are unchanged.
##   * `ST5`'s "You have any coin?" becomes a `define exchange` — the one place
##     the source's shape survives literally, since `ST6`/`ST7`/`ST8` really are
##     a yes/no/never-mind question.
##   * The patience counter (`story.ni:1937-1949`) becomes the `asked again` and
##     `asked many times` greeting arms — but it counts a different thing, and
##     the difference is measured, not assumed (driven through `sharpee play`,
##     2026-08-22). The source counts APPROACHES: every `talk to` bumps it, and
##     moving to another stall resets it. Chord counts TOPIC ASKS, cumulatively
##     and forever, and the arm is only consulted when a conversation scene
##     REOPENS — a second `talk to` inside a live scene answers "doesn't
##     respond", and the scene stays live for several turns after the player
##     walks away. So a stallkeeper you pestered stays exasperated when you come
##     back much later, where Gentry's cooled off the moment you moved on. The
##     lines are his either way; only the trigger differs.
##   * `ST9`'s good-bye is authored on the `on leaving` arm and is CURRENTLY
##     UNREACHABLE (checked the same way, and then in the source): the arm means
##     "when this character walks out on YOU" — both its consumers key on the
##     NPC's own `leave` statement — and the parser has no `goodbye`/`bye` verb
##     for the other direction. Filed as GH #300. The line stays here because
##     the arm is the right home for it the day a farewell exists; nothing else
##     depends on it.
##   * `After populating ST1` removes already-fired options from the menu.
##     That is menu bookkeeping with no parser analogue, so it is not carried;
##     re-asking simply replays the answer.
##
## THE MERCENARIES ARE ALREADY IN THE MARKET during the calm walk — they are
## just not hunting Jack yet. That is what makes `ST3` the quip the change
## document says it is, the one that lets Jack learn about them BEFORE they are
## looking for her, and it is why the stallkeeper's own description ("Armed
## thugs stomping around...") reads correctly here without a calm variant. The
## refusals that gate this tree once they start sweeping (`story.ni:1875-1879`)
## belong with the chase and are not built here.
##
## Every line below is Gentry's, carried from `story.ni:1841-1949`.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

create the grocer
  a person
  aka stallkeeper, stall keeper, merchant, seller, vendor, grocer, greengrocer
  pronouns he
  mood nervous
  in the Grocery Stall

  The stallkeeper looks nervous and irritable. Armed thugs stomping around the
  Grubber's Market is not good for business.

define greetings for the grocer
  first time:
    phrase st-opener
  on return:
    phrase st-opener
  asked again:
    phrase st-patience-second
  asked many times:
    phrase st-patience-third
  on leaving:
    phrase st-goodbye
end greetings

define topics for the grocer
  about "the wares", "the merchandise", "the goods", "what's for sale", "his stall":
    phrase st-for-sale
    then asks st-coin-grocer
  about "the mercenaries", "the thugs", "the soldiers", "the bravos":
    phrase st-mercenaries
  about "buying", "buying something", "a purchase", "money", "coin":
    phrase st-buy
    then asks st-coin-grocer
end topics

define exchange st-coin-grocer for the grocer
  answer "yes", "aye", "lots":
    phrase st-coin-yes
  answer "no", "nope", "none":
    phrase st-coin-no
  answer "never mind", "nevermind", "forget it":
    phrase st-nevermind
  on silence:
    phrase st-nevermind
end exchange

